# Token use report

Version-Timestamp: {{ version_timestamp }}

A regular look at where sessions spend tokens, so the biggest waste gets fixed first. It uses the session-report plugin ([claude-plugins.md](claude-plugins.md)).

## Run it

Once a week, from a folder outside every repository, for example `~/session-reports/`, ask Claude Code:

> Use session-report for the last 7 days.

The plugin reads the local session transcripts under `~/.claude/projects` for every project on this computer and saves an HTML file named `session-report-<date>.html` in the current folder. It sends nothing over the network; the page loads a web font when you open it.

## Keep reports local

- A report quotes prompts, file paths and project names. Never commit it, attach it to a client deliverable, paste it into an issue or publish it.
- This project's .gitignore should ignore `session-report-*.html` in case one is saved here by mistake. Confirm it with `git check-ignore -v session-report-test.html`; an adopted project may keep its own .gitignore without the rule. Move such a file out of the repository.
- Delete old reports when you no longer need them.

## Act on the top items

Read the findings at the top and the suggestions at the bottom. Fix the one or two largest items first, then compare next week's report.

| Finding | Usual fix |
| --- | --- |
| One long session carries many unrelated jobs | Start a fresh session for each independent job. Save a checkpoint and resume instead of keeping one session open. |
| Subagents use far more tokens per call than expected | Give each subagent a narrow brief: the goal, the files to read and the answer to return. Do not paste whole files into the brief. |
| Large command output or file dumps fill the context | Keep big outputs in files and read only the part you need. Prefer a targeted search to a full listing. |
| Frequent cache breaks | Read the report's cache-break list for the cause. Switching models in the middle of a session is a common one. |

Record a lasting lesson with starter-review, so the fix survives the next session.
